1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,086
Titanium alloy Ti-6Al-4V reaches 900 to
1400 megapascals tensile strength,

2
00:00:06,086 --> 00:00:11,515
documented by ASTM International across
different heat treatments.

3
00:00:11,515 --> 00:00:15,042
That exceeds many structural steels in
absolute terms.

4
00:00:15,042 --> 00:00:19,157
But the question assumes strength is one
number, and it is not.

5
00:00:19,157 --> 00:00:24,442
Titanium's reputation comes from strength
per unit mass, not raw load capacity, and

6
00:00:24,442 --> 00:00:28,517
from holding strength in environments
where steel corrodes away.

7
00:00:28,517 --> 00:00:33,192
The number you find in a handbook is the
answer to a specific test under controlled

8
00:00:33,192 --> 00:00:37,077
conditions, not a universal property that
applies in every situation.

9
00:00:37,077 --> 00:00:42,031
When you ask how strong titanium is, you
are asking for a number that does not

10
00:00:42,031 --> 00:00:43,237
exist in isolation.

11
00:00:43,237 --> 00:00:47,627
Strength is load per unit area at a
defined failure point, measured under

12
00:00:47,627 --> 00:00:48,949
controlled conditions.

13
00:00:48,949 --> 00:00:52,893
Tensile strength measures resistance to
being pulled apart.

14
00:00:52,893 --> 00:00:57,371
Yield strength is the stress at which
permanent deformation begins.

15
00:00:57,371 --> 00:01:02,332
Compressive strength, shear strength, and
fatigue strength are separate values.

16
00:01:02,332 --> 00:01:07,606
ASTM International publishes standards for
each test because the same material gives

17
00:01:07,606 --> 00:01:10,997
different numbers depending on what you
are measuring.

18
00:01:10,997 --> 00:01:16,267
The test method, specimen geometry,
loading rate, and temperature all affect

19
00:01:16,267 --> 00:01:17,029
the result.

20
00:01:17,029 --> 00:01:22,937
Commercially pure titanium, unalloyed, has
tensile strength between 240 and 550

21
00:01:22,937 --> 00:01:27,051
megapascals depending on oxygen content
and processing.

22
00:01:27,051 --> 00:01:33,462
The workhorse alloy Ti-6Al-4V, six percent
aluminum and four percent vanadium,

23
00:01:33,462 --> 00:01:38,395
reaches 900 to 1400 megapascals depending
on heat treatment.

24
00:01:38,395 --> 00:01:43,032
Mild steel sits around 400 to 550
megapascals.

25
00:01:43,032 --> 00:01:48,779
High-strength steel alloys reach 760 to
1400 megapascals.

26
00:01:48,779 --> 00:01:53,626
In absolute tensile strength, titanium
alloys overlap with steel alloys.

27
00:01:53,626 --> 00:01:55,376
The difference is density.

28
00:01:55,376 --> 00:02:00,930
A titanium bar and a steel bar of equal
cross-section can carry similar loads, but

29
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:03,843
the titanium bar weighs substantially
less.

30
00:02:03,843 --> 00:02:10,707
Titanium has a density of 4.5 grams per
cubic centimeter. Steel is 7.85.

31
00:02:10,707 --> 00:02:15,116
When you divide tensile strength by
density, you get specific strength, the

32
00:02:15,116 --> 00:02:18,232
load a material can carry per unit of its
own weight.

33
00:02:18,232 --> 00:02:23,955
Ti-6Al-4V delivers 204 to 317
kilonewton-meters per kilogram.

34
00:02:23,955 --> 00:02:27,613
High-strength steel delivers 97 to 179.

35
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:32,331
Aluminum alloy 7075, another aerospace
material, reaches 193.

36
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:38,287
Titanium alloys carry more load per
kilogram than steel or aluminum, which is

37
00:02:38,287 --> 00:02:44,397
why they appear in aircraft frames and
turbine blades where every gram matters.

38
00:02:44,397 --> 00:02:48,979
The advantage is not that titanium is
stronger in absolute terms, but that it

39
00:02:48,979 --> 00:02:51,955
delivers comparable strength at much lower
weight.

40
00:02:51,955 --> 00:02:53,790
This is not theoretical.

41
00:02:53,790 --> 00:02:59,907
The Lockheed SR-71, which flew at Mach 3
and endured skin temperatures above 300

42
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:05,795
degrees Celsius, used titanium alloy for
85 percent of its structural weight.

43
00:03:05,795 --> 00:03:09,706
Steel would have been too heavy. Aluminum
would have softened.

44
00:03:09,706 --> 00:03:14,248
The choice was made on specific strength
and thermal stability together.

45
00:03:14,248 --> 00:03:20,252
Boeing 787 and Airbus A350 airframes use
titanium in landing gear and engine mounts

46
00:03:20,252 --> 00:03:21,699
for the same reason.

47
00:03:21,699 --> 00:03:26,707
The parts must carry high loads without
adding unnecessary mass to the aircraft.

48
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:29,608
ASTM standard E8 defines the tensile test.

49
00:03:29,608 --> 00:03:35,203
A machined specimen, usually cylindrical
or flat with a reduced gauge section, is

50
00:03:35,203 --> 00:03:38,104
gripped at both ends in a testing machine.

51
00:03:38,104 --> 00:03:42,602
The machine pulls at a controlled rate
while a strain gauge or extensometer

52
00:03:42,602 --> 00:03:43,741
records elongation.

53
00:03:43,741 --> 00:03:47,518
Stress is load divided by original
cross-sectional area.

54
00:03:47,518 --> 00:03:50,755
Strain is elongation divided by original
length.

55
00:03:50,755 --> 00:03:56,310
The specimen stretches elastically, then
plastically, then necks down and

56
00:03:56,310 --> 00:04:00,608
The peak stress before fracture is the
ultimate tensile strength.

57
00:04:00,608 --> 00:04:04,933
The stress at the onset of plastic
deformation is the yield strength.

58
00:04:04,933 --> 00:04:09,752
Every published titanium strength value
traces to a test like this, performed on a

59
00:04:09,752 --> 00:04:14,101
specimen with documented composition, heat
treatment, and grain structure.

60
00:04:14,101 --> 00:04:18,611
The range in published values reflects
real variation in processing, not

61
00:04:18,611 --> 00:04:19,739
measurement error.

62
00:04:19,739 --> 00:04:24,651
A specimen annealed at 700 degrees Celsius
will have different grain size and

63
00:04:24,651 --> 00:04:29,945
dislocation density than one annealed at
900 degrees Celsius, and the strength will

64
00:04:29,945 --> 00:04:31,157
differ accordingly.

65
00:04:31,157 --> 00:04:36,083
The test is repeatable, but the material
itself varies with processing history.

66
00:04:36,083 --> 00:04:40,515
I kept trying to land on one number for
'how strong,' because that's what the

67
00:04:40,515 --> 00:04:41,379
title promises.

68
00:04:41,379 --> 00:04:42,525
There isn't one.

69
00:04:42,525 --> 00:04:48,183
Tensile, yield, specific and fatigue
strength are four different answers, and I

70
00:04:48,183 --> 00:04:49,544
wrote them as four.

71
00:04:49,544 --> 00:04:54,563
Yield strength is the stress at which a
material begins to deform permanently.

72
00:04:54,563 --> 00:04:59,219
Below yield, the material returns to its
original shape when the load is removed.

73
00:04:59,219 --> 00:05:01,094
Above yield, it stays bent.

74
00:05:01,094 --> 00:05:06,719
Engineers design most structures to stay
below yield strength under normal loads,

75
00:05:06,719 --> 00:05:09,219
not below ultimate tensile strength.

76
00:05:09,219 --> 00:05:14,870
Ti-6Al-4V has a yield strength of 830 to
1100 megapascals depending on heat

77
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:15,624
treatment.

78
00:05:15,624 --> 00:05:19,728
That is the number that governs whether a
part will hold its shape in service.

79
00:05:19,728 --> 00:05:24,421
Ultimate tensile strength tells you the
breaking point, but yield strength tells

80
00:05:24,421 --> 00:05:27,179
you the limit of elastic, recoverable
behavior.

81
00:05:27,179 --> 00:05:31,123
Ultimate tensile strength tells you when
the material will fracture.

82
00:05:31,123 --> 00:05:34,139
Yield strength tells you when it stops
being useful.

83
00:05:34,139 --> 00:05:39,083
The gap between them is ductility, the
ability to deform before breaking.

84
00:05:39,083 --> 00:05:45,634
Titanium alloys are moderately ductile,
with elongation at fracture between 10 and

85
00:05:45,634 --> 00:05:47,632
15 percent for Ti-6Al-4V.

86
00:05:47,632 --> 00:05:52,880
That is less than mild steel, which can
exceed 25 percent, but enough to prevent

87
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,389
sudden brittle failure.

88
00:05:54,389 --> 00:05:59,845
A ductile material gives warning before it
breaks. A brittle material does not.

89
00:05:59,845 --> 00:06:04,221
Titanium sits in the middle, offering
reasonable ductility without the excessive

90
00:06:04,221 --> 00:06:08,213
deformation that would make a part
unusable before it actually fractures.

91
00:06:08,213 --> 00:06:13,610
Fatigue is failure under repeated loading,
even when each individual load is below

92
00:06:13,610 --> 00:06:14,597
yield strength.

93
00:06:14,597 --> 00:06:19,872
A titanium component in a jet engine
experiences millions of stress cycles.

94
00:06:19,872 --> 00:06:23,313
ASTM E466 defines the rotating-beam
fatigue test.

95
00:06:23,313 --> 00:06:28,931
A specimen is subjected to cyclic stress,
and the number of cycles to failure is

96
00:06:28,931 --> 00:06:29,563
recorded.

97
00:06:29,563 --> 00:06:33,458
The result is an S-N curve, stress versus
number of cycles.

98
00:06:33,458 --> 00:06:37,024
For titanium alloys, there is no true
endurance limit.

99
00:06:37,024 --> 00:06:41,968
Fatigue cracks can initiate even at low
stress if the cycle count is high enough.

100
00:06:41,968 --> 00:06:47,048
Steel sometimes shows an endurance limit
below which fatigue cracks do not

101
00:06:47,048 --> 00:06:52,608
propagate. Titanium does not. Every stress
cycle contributes to eventual failure.

102
00:06:52,608 --> 00:06:57,859
Ti-6Al-4V shows fatigue strength around
240 to 500 megapascals at ten million

103
00:06:57,859 --> 00:07:02,906
cycles, but the range is wide because
surface finish, residual stress, and

104
00:07:02,906 --> 00:07:04,475
environment all matter.

105
00:07:04,475 --> 00:07:09,664
A machined surface with tool marks
concentrates stress and lowers fatigue

106
00:07:09,664 --> 00:07:12,706
A polished or shot-peened surface raises
it.

107
00:07:12,706 --> 00:07:15,472
Saltwater or high temperature lowers it.

108
00:07:15,472 --> 00:07:18,521
The published range reflects this
sensitivity.

109
00:07:18,521 --> 00:07:23,360
Fatigue is not a material property in the
same sense as tensile strength.

110
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:28,401
It is a system property that depends on
geometry, loading history, and environment

111
00:07:28,401 --> 00:07:30,491
as much as on the material itself.

112
00:07:30,491 --> 00:07:35,855
Titanium forms a tenacious oxide layer,
primarily titanium dioxide, within

113
00:07:35,855 --> 00:07:38,827
milliseconds of exposure to air or water.

114
00:07:38,827 --> 00:07:44,236
This layer is self-healing and protects
the underlying metal from further

115
00:07:44,236 --> 00:07:46,533
oxidation in most environments.

116
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:51,325
Steel corrodes in seawater and loses
load-bearing cross-section over time.

117
00:07:51,325 --> 00:07:52,491
Titanium does not.

118
00:07:52,491 --> 00:07:57,054
The effective strength of a titanium part
in a marine environment remains near its

119
00:07:57,054 --> 00:07:58,501
initial value for decades.

120
00:07:58,501 --> 00:08:02,269
The effective strength of a steel part
drops as corrosion progresses.

121
00:08:02,269 --> 00:08:06,474
The initial strength of steel may be
higher, but the strength after ten years

122
00:08:06,474 --> 00:08:08,112
of seawater exposure is lower.

123
00:08:08,112 --> 00:08:11,989
Titanium maintains its strength because it
maintains its cross-section.

124
00:08:11,989 --> 00:08:17,067
This is why titanium is used in chemical
processing, desalination plants, and

125
00:08:17,067 --> 00:08:20,496
offshore structures despite its higher
initial cost.

126
00:08:20,496 --> 00:08:23,933
The strength you measure in a lab persists
in service.

127
00:08:23,933 --> 00:08:29,151
For steel, you must either accept strength
loss or add corrosion protection, which

128
00:08:29,151 --> 00:08:30,933
adds weight and maintenance.

129
00:08:30,933 --> 00:08:34,259
Titanium's corrosion resistance is not
absolute.

130
00:08:34,259 --> 00:08:37,723
Hydrochloric acid and hydrofluoric acid
attack it.

131
00:08:37,723 --> 00:08:41,131
Dry chlorine gas at elevated temperature
attacks it.

132
00:08:41,131 --> 00:08:46,112
But in the environments where most
engineering structures operate, seawater,

133
00:08:46,112 --> 00:08:50,176
freshwater, and atmospheric exposure,
titanium remains stable.

134
00:08:50,176 --> 00:08:54,955
Ti-6Al-4V retains useful strength up to
about 600 degrees Celsius.

135
00:08:54,955 --> 00:09:00,602
Above that, oxidation accelerates and the
alloy begins to absorb oxygen, which

136
00:09:00,602 --> 00:09:01,616
embrittles it.

137
00:09:01,616 --> 00:09:04,978
Aluminum alloys soften above 150 degrees
Celsius.

138
00:09:04,978 --> 00:09:09,919
Steel retains strength to higher
temperatures than titanium, but at much

139
00:09:09,919 --> 00:09:10,880
higher weight.

140
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:16,967
The SR-71 example is real because titanium
occupies a thermal niche where aluminum

141
00:09:16,967 --> 00:09:19,120
fails and steel is too heavy.

142
00:09:19,120 --> 00:09:23,461
The airframe skin reached temperatures
where aluminum would have lost structural

143
00:09:23,461 --> 00:09:26,880
integrity, but titanium maintained both
strength and stiffness.

144
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:31,823
Newer titanium aluminides, intermetallic
compounds rather than solid-solution

145
00:09:31,823 --> 00:09:35,547
alloys, push the temperature limit to 750
degrees Celsius.

146
00:09:35,547 --> 00:09:40,560
These are used in the last stages of jet
engine compressors, where titanium alloys

147
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:42,883
would oxidize and aluminum would melt.

148
00:09:42,883 --> 00:09:44,884
The trade-off is brittleness.

149
00:09:44,884 --> 00:09:50,266
Titanium aluminides are less ductile than
Ti-6Al-4V, which limits their use to

150
00:09:50,266 --> 00:09:54,267
components that do not experience impact
or bending loads.

151
00:09:54,267 --> 00:09:58,269
The temperature capability comes at the
cost of toughness.

152
00:09:58,269 --> 00:10:00,890
Watch for the absence of a single answer.

153
00:10:00,890 --> 00:10:06,130
The script states tensile strength, yield
strength, specific strength, and fatigue

154
00:10:06,130 --> 00:10:09,389
strength as separate values with separate
meanings.

155
00:10:09,389 --> 00:10:13,144
Each is tied to a test standard and a
documented range.

156
00:10:13,144 --> 00:10:18,402
The magnitude comparisons use real
published figures for titanium, steel, and

157
00:10:18,402 --> 00:10:19,016
aluminum.

158
00:10:19,016 --> 00:10:22,818
The SR-71 example is historical fact, not
anecdote.

159
00:10:22,818 --> 00:10:28,632
The Boeing 787 and Airbus A350 references
are current production aircraft with

160
00:10:28,632 --> 00:10:34,893
documented titanium use. Every number in a
directive appears in the narration first.

161
00:10:34,893 --> 00:10:39,092
The published strength ranges come from
standardized tests on specimens with

162
00:10:39,092 --> 00:10:41,192
controlled composition and processing.

163
00:10:41,192 --> 00:10:45,472
They do not account for defects,
contamination, or non-standard heat

164
00:10:45,472 --> 00:10:47,864
treatment in a given production batch.

165
00:10:47,864 --> 00:10:52,324
Real parts can fall outside the published
range if processing deviates.

166
00:10:52,324 --> 00:10:57,600
The numbers are design values, not
guarantees for every piece of titanium

167
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:03,090
ASTM and ASM International publish the
standards and databases, but they document

168
00:11:03,090 --> 00:11:05,733
what is typical, not what is universal.

169
00:11:05,733 --> 00:11:09,497
A casting with porosity will be weaker
than a wrought bar.

170
00:11:09,497 --> 00:11:14,688
A part with surface cracks will fail in
fatigue sooner than a polished specimen.

171
00:11:14,688 --> 00:11:20,155
The test data represent ideal conditions,
and real service conditions are not ideal.

172
00:11:20,155 --> 00:11:24,290
Titanium is strong in the sense that
matters for aerospace and marine

173
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:28,665
engineering: high strength per unit
weight, stable over time in corrosive

174
00:11:28,665 --> 00:11:32,501
environments, and usable at temperatures
where aluminum softens.

175
00:11:32,501 --> 00:11:35,446
The absolute tensile strength overlaps
with steel.

176
00:11:35,446 --> 00:11:40,218
The advantage is carrying that strength at
nearly half the weight, and keeping it

177
00:11:40,218 --> 00:11:41,808
when steel would rust away.
